chx:F1 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Background"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Chapter 20 of the 3rd edition, Sending Email, Texts, and Push Notifications, teaches three ways for a program to reach a person: the Gmail API through the EZGmail module (setup with a credentials file and a token file, send() with attachments, cc and bcc, GmailThread and GmailMessage objects from unread(), recent() and search(), and the download of attachments), texts through the SMS email gateways of Table 20-1 with Twilio named as the reliable alternative, and push notifications through ntfy with the Requests library (a POST to a topic, the headers Title, Tags and Priority, and a poll with /json?poll=1 and since); it ends with five practice questions and three practice programs, Umbrella Reminder, Auto Unsubscriber and Email-Based Computer Control [P01]. The page was fetched on 2026-10-13 and returned 200 with 53,147 bytes, and its sha256 equals the chapterDigest of the book's own chapter ontology [P01] [P03] [P04]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P03, chx:P04 .
chx:F2 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "The online materials"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The online materials of the 3rd edition (7,759,877 bytes, 91 files, 72 of them Python) hold no file for chapter 20: no program names ezgmail, ntfy, smtplib, imaplib, Twilio or weather.gov, and phoneAndEmail.py in them belongs to chapter 8 [P02]. The chapter's last sentence sends a reader who is stuck on the Email-Based Computer Control program to the book page of No Starch Press for an example implementation, but that page has 0 occurrences of an example implementation, of Email-Based, of ezgmail or of ntfy [P01] [P04]. The 2nd edition's chapter 18 mentions smtplib 21 times, imapclient 20, pyzmail 14 and Twilio 31, which the 3rd edition's chapter 20 replaces with the Gmail API, the gateway table and ntfy, and it names smtplib and imaplib only to say that EZGmail is better [P01] [P07]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P02, chx:P04, chx:P07 .
chx:F3 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "The chapter replayed"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The 48 statements of the chapter's 15 listings were replayed as printed in three environments. With ezgmail 2026.4.28 and no credentials, which is where a reader stands before the Google Cloud steps, 19 of 48 matched the book, and 19 matched with the Requests 2.32.3 that Appendix A pins; in an environment with neither package 3 matched; ezgmail.init() and every call after it raised EZGmailException (Can't find credentials file at DIR/credentials.json.) and ezgmail.LOGGED_IN stayed False [P01] [P05] [P08]. On a stand-in for the server of Google, whose replies were written by this study, 42 of 46 matched in the zone America/Los_Angeles and 38 of 46 in Europe/Istanbul, where the dates printed Dec 10 instead of Dec 09 and the timestamp read 2026-12-10 00:28:48 [P01]. The four that do not match in either zone are the send() of listing 4 and the Popen of listing 15, whose quoted strings the web page breaks across lines (SyntaxError: unterminated string literal), the printed text of str(unread_threads[0]), and the Popen of a Windows path with its line break repaired (FileNotFoundError on Linux) [P01]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P05, chx:P08 .
chx:F4 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "The printed thread text"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter prints str(unread_threads[0]) as a GmailThread with len=2 and a snippet that has no opening quote, but ezgmail 2026.4.28 prints <GmailThread numMessages=2 snippet='Do you want to watch RoboCop this weekend?'>, and building that text costs a request to the API, because the thread fetches its messages the first time messages is read [P01] [P08] [P09]. The printed summary line ends in Dec 09 and the printed timestamp is datetime.datetime(2026, 12, 9, 13, 28, 48), but the package builds timestamp as a naive datetime from internalDate, so the same message reads 08:48:48 in America/Chicago, 14:48:48 in UTC and 17:48:48 in Europe/Istanbul; the chapter never says that the reading depends on the zone of the computer [P01]. The example fromtimestamp(1797823712) printed as 2026-12-20 21:28:32 is right only in a zone at UTC-6; in UTC it is 2026-12-21 03:28:32, a Monday [P01]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P08, chx:P09 .
chx:F5 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Credentials, token and expiry"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The page of the chapter never uses the file name credentials.json (it says a credentials file and a token file and names token.json), although Appendix B does, and it never uses the word refresh [P01] [P06]. import ezgmail already calls init(_raiseException=False), so a failure of login is hidden until the first call; a token.json written by oauth2client is plain JSON of 16 keys with the file mode 0o600 and holds the client secret and the refresh token, so the chapter's advice to treat it like a password is right [P08] [P09]. Google's OAuth page says that a project whose consent screen is external and in the publishing status Testing is issued a refresh token that expires in 7 days, which the chapter does not say and which this study read but could not run, because it needs a Google account and a week [P48]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P06, chx:P08, chx:P09, chx:P48 .
chx:F6 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "The library under EZGmail"@en ; sen0414:findingText "ezgmail 2026.4.28 (uploaded 2026-04-28) requires google-api-python-client and oauth2client; oauth2client 4.1.3 was uploaded on 2018-09-07 and is classified Development Status 7 - Inactive, while google-api-python-client was at 2.201.0, uploaded 2026-09-30, so the login of the chapter's library stands on an authorisation package that has not changed in eight years [P09] [P13]. The package declares no Requires-Python in its metadata, and under Python 3.14.6 it was imported and ran through the stand-in [P09]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P09, chx:P13 .
chx:F7 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Defects of EZGmail found by execution"@en ; sen0414:findingText "On the stand-in, reply() posted a message with the threadId of the original but with the subject unchanged (RoboCop, no Re:) and without In-Reply-To or References, which Google's sending guide names, together with a matching Subject, as what makes a message join a thread, and replyAll() made no API call and returned None [P49]. downloadAllAttachments wrote an attachment named ../evil.txt one level above the download folder, and downloadAttachment overwrote an existing file as the chapter says [P01]. A message with no Subject header raised AttributeError on .subject and on str(); summary() cut the last character of a sender written without a space (noreply@example.com became noreply@example.co); removeQuotedParts cut only the English form On Sun, Jan 1, 2018 at 12:00 PM ... wrote: and left the replies of the forms On Sun, 1 Jan 2018 at 12:00, a Turkish line, and the English line with U+202F before PM; and a subject with a line break raised HeaderParseError before any request was made [P08] [P09]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P08, chx:P09, chx:P49 .
chx:F8 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Non-ASCII text and bytes"@en ; sen0414:findingText "ezgmail sent a Turkish body as charset utf-8 in base64 and a Turkish subject as an encoded word, and both came back unchanged [P36] [P37]. Requests sent a str body as UTF-8 (21 bytes for the 20 characters of Yedekleme tamamlandı) with no Content-Type, so the receiver has to assume the charset, and refused a header value with a dotless i with UnicodeEncodeError because it encodes header values as latin-1, and a header given as the integer 5 with InvalidHeader, so a Title with a Turkish word fails while the book's English Title and its Priority string work [P01] [P10]. The limit of ntfy is 4,096 bytes, not characters: 4096 ASCII characters fit and 2049 dotless i characters are 4098 bytes, and the documentation says a longer message is treated as an attachment, which the chapter does not say [P15]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P10, chx:P15, chx:P36, chx:P37 .
chx:F9 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "The gateways have gone or are going"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Table 20-1 of the chapter begins with the gateways txt.att.net and mms.att.net, and AT&T's support article says that on June 17, 2025 its email-to-text and text-to-email service was shut down, 483 days before 2026-10-13 and a Tuesday [P01] [P58]. Verizon's notice says it has begun to shut down email-to-text for Vtext.com and VZWPix.com, expects to complete it by 03/31/2027, a Wednesday 169 days after 2026-10-13, and that some senders may lose access earlier, so the gateway of the chapter's own example, 2125551234@vtext.com, is on a schedule to end [P59]. A DNS-over-HTTPS query for the MX records of the 18 domains of the table found no MX for txt.att.net, mms.att.net and sms.cricketwireless.net, NXDOMAIN for messaging.sprintpcs.com and pm.sprint.com and SERVFAIL for sms.myboostmobile.com, and mail exchangers for the other 12; a missing MX is a symptom, not proof [P01]. Twilio's REST API answered a request with no credentials with HTTP 401 (code 20003), and nothing else about Twilio could be observed without an account [P61]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P58, chx:P59, chx:P61 .
chx:F10 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "How long a text can be"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter says an SMS gateway limits a message to 160 characters; Twilio's page explains the arithmetic, 140 bytes of 8 bits at 7 bits a character, and that a character such as { needs an escape and counts as two [P01] [P60]. Counted with a GSM-7 table written for the study, 160 characters of the basic set fit one message and 161 need 2, {x} costs 5, and one character outside the table, the dotless i of Yedekleme tamamlandı, turns the text into UCS-2 at 16 bits a character, so 70 characters fit and 71 need 2 [P60]. The segment sizes for longer texts, 153 and 67 characters, follow from a 6-byte header and were not found on a page opened by this study; they are unconfirmed [P60]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P60 .
chx:F11 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "ntfy as the chapter uses it"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter's four event values, message, open, keepalive and poll_request, are two fewer than the six in the documentation, which adds message_delete and message_clear; the example answer has expires 43200 seconds, 12 hours, after time [P01] [P16]. The poll answer is not valid JSON as a whole (json.loads raised Extra data) but one object per line, and a blank line raised JSONDecodeError again, so a program that splits lines should skip empty ones [P01]. The random part of the book's topic, ZPgxBQ42, is 8 characters of 62, 47.6 bits, and the other half is the author's name; ntfy's FAQ says that without access control the topic name is the password, and the topic is part of the URL path, so it appears in logs [P18]. post() returns a Response whose ok was False on a 429 that a recorder returned, with nothing raised, so a program that notifies about a long job fails silently when a limit is reached; only raise_for_status() raised [P01] [P10]. GET /v1/health answered 200 with healthy true on the day and a poll of an unused topic answered 200 with an empty body; nothing was published to ntfy.sh [P19]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P10, chx:P16, chx:P18, chx:P19 .
chx:F12 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "The from address and what the chapter leaves out"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter says it is fairly trivial for hackers to fake a from address and asks for a password in the mail [P01]. On a local server the envelope sender bounces@mallory.example and the header From: CEO <ceo@bank.example> were independent, with no login and no TLS, and a Bcc recipient was in the envelope but not in the text, as the documentation of send_message says [P20] [P34] [P35]. The page of the chapter never uses the words STARTTLS, TLS, SSL, SPF, DKIM, DMARC, Reply-To, Message-ID, encoding or timeout [P01]. The SPF record of gmail.com on the day was v=spf1 redirect=_spf.google.com, its DMARC record v=DMARC1; p=none; sp=quarantine; rua=mailto:mailauth-reports@google.com, and Google's sender guidelines require authentication from senders of more than 5,000 messages a day [P44] [P45] [P57]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P20, chx:P34, chx:P35, chx:P44, chx:P45, chx:P57 .
chx:F13 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "What changed in Python and in the standard library"@en ; sen0414:findingText "One program that exercises email, smtplib, imaplib, ssl and email.utils against a small SMTP server written with sockets was run under 11 interpreters (3.11.17, 3.12.3, 3.13.16, 3.14.0 to 3.14.6 and 3.15.0b4) and compared 48 items; 19 differ between releases [P29] [P30] [P31] [P32]. imaplib.IMAP4.idle() exists from 3.14 only; smtplib sends its commands in lower case from 3.13 (mail from:, rcpt to:) where 3.12 sent mail FROM:; the keyfile and certfile arguments of starttls and of the SSL classes are gone from 3.12; ssl.create_default_context() adds VERIFY_X509_STRICT and VERIFY_X509_PARTIAL_CHAIN from 3.13, while its minimum of TLS 1.2 is older (3.11.17 already reports TLS 1.2, the documentation dates the TLS 1.2 minimum of the SSL contexts to 3.10, and the Ubuntu 3.12.3 package reports MINIMUM_SUPPORTED, the lowest) [P27]; smtpd, asyncore and asynchat are gone from 3.12 and cgi, imghdr, sndhdr, nntplib and telnetlib from 3.13; and email.utils.localtime(isdst=) is gone from 3.14 [P20] [P21] [P33]. parseaddr returned empty strings for two addresses in one string in every interpreter of the study, and the 3.14 documentation says its strict argument came in 3.13 [P25]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P20, chx:P21, chx:P25, chx:P29, chx:P30, chx:P31, chx:P32, chx:P33 .
chx:F14 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "The practice programs"@en ; sen0414:findingText "A search of the forecast text for the word rain found it in neither of 2 sample periods and so missed the period with a chance of precipitation of 60, which a comparison of the probabilityOfPrecipitation number with a limit flagged; api.weather.gov returned 14 periods for a point in Chicago with such a number in each [P01] [P63]. A parser for links whose text says unsubscribe found 1 of 3 candidates, because an image link and the text opt out are missed, while the List-Unsubscribe header (RFC 2369) with List-Unsubscribe-Post (RFC 8058) gives a mailto: and an https: address without parsing HTML [P42] [P43]. The control loop ran only the commands of an allow-list, so rm was refused, and a subject run through a shell (status; echo PWNED) printed an injected line until it was passed as a list argument; hmac.compare_digest raised TypeError for a non-ASCII str, and the book's Popen of qBittorrent raised FileNotFoundError on Linux [P01]. Mail was received with SMTP and read with IMAP, deleted and expunged against dovecot 2.3.21, a local stand-in for Gmail [P38]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P38, chx:P42, chx:P43, chx:P63 .
chx:F15 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Not done in this study"@en ; sen0414:findingText "No Gmail account, Google Cloud project, phone number, carrier, Twilio account or ntfy subscriber was used: nothing was sent to a person, a phone or a push service, and a connection to imap.gmail.com and smtp.gmail.com timed out from this machine, so no Gmail server was reached [P69] [P70]. The Gmail replies were written by this study, the SMTP server was aiosmtpd, the IMAP server was dovecot 2.3.21 and the ntfy side was a recorder on 127.0.0.1, all labelled stand-ins; the real ntfy server could not be downloaded here, github.com answering 403. The 7-day expiry of a Testing refresh token, the acceptance of .exe and .zip attachments, the delivery of a text through a gateway, the 153 and 67 character segments and the behaviour of a real Gmail inbox are therefore not verified [P48] [P55]. No photograph is used, because the single request to Wikimedia Commons answered 429." ; sen0414:cites chx:P48, chx:P55, chx:P69, chx:P70 .
chx:Scorecard a sen0414:QualityScorecard ; rdfs:label "Quality scorecard"@en ; sen0414:topicCoverage "34 primary-source concepts inventoried from the textbook ontology." ; sen0414:citationStrength "72 sources, all opened and verified at 2026-10-13T12:00:00; 43 cited by findings." ; sen0414:methodologyCompliance "RDODI procedure v1.6.0 Stage 1." ; sen0414:reproducibility "Every source is a stable public URL; every claim names its sources." .
